WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.240
Vsauce! Kevin here, with two known criminals&nbsp;
-- and we have to find out who’s most likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.520
to reoffend. Is it the teenage girl who stole&nbsp;
a neighbor’s scooter and rode it around her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.120
neighborhood before dropping it on a lawn and&nbsp;
walking away? Or the 41-year old male who’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:24.080
already been convicted of armed robbery? Who do&nbsp;
you think is more likely to commit another crime?

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:31.600
The answer is: It doesn’t matter who YOU&nbsp;
choose. Algorithms are being used to determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:38.400
future criminals. One widely used in the United&nbsp;
States is likely to label the scooter thief as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.840
a higher risk than the actual violent&nbsp;
felon -- and the Supreme Court decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:57.840
if the algorithm gets it wrong… it doesn’t care.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:07.760
That scenario is actually real -- on a scale&nbsp;
of 1 to 10 measuring a criminal’s likelihood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.160
of recidivism -- whether they’re gonna&nbsp;
commit more crimes in the future -- the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:18.720
teen scooter thief was rated an 8. The&nbsp;
seasoned armed robber was rated a 3.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:25.760
Predicting who will commit a crime has been an&nbsp;
inexact science for centuries. In the late 1700s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:31.680
Viennese psychologist Franz Joseph Gall ushered&nbsp;
in the era of phrenology -- the theory that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:38.880
bumps on your head predicted personality traits.&nbsp;
George Combe even identified 3 skull shapes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:45.200
not only predicted criminality, but determined&nbsp;
whether the person should be held responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.080
for their criminal actions. But do you&nbsp;
know who’s most likely to commit a crime?

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.480
People who are already criminals.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:57.760
It’s common sense to factor criminal&nbsp;
history into sentencing to protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.640
society -- the more crime you’ve committed,&nbsp;
the higher the likelihood that you’ll commit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:09.840
more -- so the more time you spend in&nbsp;
prison. But judges and juries are human.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:15.280
It can be difficult to tell whether&nbsp;
someone can be rehabilitated or whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:22.400
they’ve already learned their lesson. And&nbsp;
we can’t just guess. There should be a fair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:29.040
unbiased risk assessment algorithm to&nbsp;
counter the shortcomings of human judgment.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.840
What we’ve got might as well&nbsp;
be head bumps and skull shapes.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:39.280
Equivant’s website says, “Software for&nbsp;
justice. You make decisions that matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:46.000
we make software to support them. We're advancing&nbsp;
justice one step at a time.” When the company was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.720
known as Northpointe, they developed COMPAS&nbsp;
software: Correctional Offender Management&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:57.280
Profiling for Alternative Sanctions. It’s one&nbsp;
of many risk assessment tools used by New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:03.840
California, and a range of municipalities to help&nbsp;
courts assess an offender’s risk of recidivism.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.800
The assessment works like this:

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:11.440
Defendants are asked over a hundred&nbsp;
questions about their criminal history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:16.400
family and educational background, work&nbsp;
history, and lifestyle -- all things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.920
that a century of social science research&nbsp;
in psychology and criminology have shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:29.200
to correlate with antisocial behavior. COMPAS’s&nbsp;
result is a simple score on a 10-point scale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.960
with 10 being the highest risk of reoffending.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:37.920
EVERYONE has an actual COMPAS&nbsp;
risk assessment – including me!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.200
Let’s find out how likely I am to commit a crime.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:46.000
Real quick let me tell you how I lost 5 pounds&nbsp;
by basically playing a math game. What? Yeah!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.240
About a month ago Noom reached out&nbsp;
about sponsoring Vsauce2 and I said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:56.640
sure but only if I can test it out. So here’s&nbsp;
what I do. I told Noom I wanted to lose 5lbs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.840
and it gave me a calorie target for each&nbsp;
day. I weigh in on the scale each morning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:07.280
I log what I eat for breakfast lunch dinner&nbsp;
and some snacks. And I follow the goal. They do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:13.040
have coaches to help you out but for me – I just&nbsp;
follow the numbers. You can also track your steps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.560
track your water intake, and just get an&nbsp;
overall numerical sense of your daily health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:24.000
and goals. I’ve tried tracking this stuff myself&nbsp;
and it never worked. Noom makes it easy to stick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.440
to the plan. If you want to try a new way to get&nbsp;
healthy, then take your FREE 30-second quiz right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:36.320
now. Noom dot com slash vsauce2. Link down in&nbsp;
the description below. That’s Noom dot com slash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:42.560
vsauce2. Use my link then they know that I sent&nbsp;
you and thanks to them for sponsoring Vsauce2.&nbsp;

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:48.240
Okay. So am I a danger to society?&nbsp;
Let’s take this test and find out.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.880
Arrests for violent offenses, no. Drug&nbsp;
trafficking, no. Prison, probation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.520
parole, gang member, no. This seems pretty&nbsp;
straightforward -- and saying yes to any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.640
of those would probably predict criminal behavior.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:04.400
“Do you live with friends?” No… but what if I did?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.840
I used to. Lots of people live with&nbsp;
their friends, because… they’re friends.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:15.520
“I always behaved myself in school,” agree or&nbsp;
disagree -- I mean… most of the time, yeah.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:22.080
Definitely. And I didn’t do anything crazy. I did&nbsp;
get detention once for writing an inappropriate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:28.320
poem. It really was not a very nice poem…&nbsp;
But does that poem really have any bearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:34.640
on whether I’ll be a criminal as an adult? What&nbsp;
delinquent mistake did you make as a student?

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:41.200
“Can you verify your employer?” Yes. “How&nbsp;
often do you worry about financial survival?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:47.120
Often, Sometimes, or Never? Well I would think&nbsp;
almost literally everyone would say Often.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.160
How often do you feel bored? Do you feel&nbsp;
discouraged at times? Is it difficult for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:57.360
you to keep your mind on one thing for&nbsp;
a long time? Have I felt sad in my life?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:00.960
Have I played sick to get out of&nbsp;
something? To get ahead in life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:06.160
do you have to put yourself first?&nbsp;
Do I always “practice what I preach?”

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:12.960
This is starting to get… kind of weird. Lots&nbsp;
of creative people feel bored or sad sometimes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.120
What does any of this really indicate?

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:22.400
And this “Criminal Attitudes” section has a&nbsp;
lot of questions that have a range of valid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:28.640
philosophical answers -- like whether the law&nbsp;
helps average people, whether a hungry person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:35.760
has a right to steal, or whether victimless crimes&nbsp;
like recreational drug use are actually crimes.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:39.920
To get my score, my answers to&nbsp;
these 137-questions would be fed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:45.280
into an expertly-developed algorithm&nbsp;
that looks something like this.... ]

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:50.880
A total black box. No one&nbsp;
knows how the algorithm works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:56.320
At all. We do know that their formula for&nbsp;
assessing the risk of violent recidivism is:

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:01.760
Risk Score S equals current age A plus the&nbsp;
age at their first arrest plus history of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:08.640
violence H plus vocational education scale plus&nbsp;
history of noncompliance, with every single term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.440
adjusted by a negative or positive weighted&nbsp;
multiplier. Oh and according to COMPAS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:18.160
weight given to multipliers is quote, “determined&nbsp;
by the strength of the item’s relationship to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:23.920
person offense recidivism that we observed in&nbsp;
our study data.” Makes sense, right? WRONG!

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.640
If you’d like an explanation of how&nbsp;
that determines someone’s violent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:32.640
recidivism rate, guess what? So did Eric Loomis.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:38.400
In Loomis v. The State of Wisconsin, Eric Loomis&nbsp;
pleaded guilty to fleeing a traffic officer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:44.160
and operating a vehicle without the owner’s&nbsp;
consent. He protested that his due process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:50.400
rights were violated by the black box that is&nbsp;
the COMPAS algorithm. He was sentenced to 6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:56.240
years in prison -- influenced in part by his&nbsp;
COMPAS rating -- and his appeal went all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:01.920
way to the US Supreme Court. They declined&nbsp;
to hear his case, and the innerworkings of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:07.360
COMPAS continue to be proprietary and&nbsp;
protected by law as a trade secret.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:12.800
The United States Sentencing Commission,&nbsp;
which I highlighted in my last video, also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:20.400
declined to investigate whether risk assessment&nbsp;
algorithms were valid -- but ProPublica did.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:26.240
They highlighted that ridiculous outcome&nbsp;
of the scooter girl, Brisha Borden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:32.320
and armed robber Vernon Prater. Their&nbsp;
investigation found that COMPAS was spectacularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:38.160
inaccurate by under-predicting the rate of&nbsp;
reoffense by white defendants and over-predicting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:44.640
recidivism by black defendants. 2 years later,&nbsp;
Borden hadn't committed any more crimes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:51.680
And Prater was serving an 8-year prison term for&nbsp;
stealing thousands of dollars of electronics.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:54.320
But the problem isn’t just COMPAS.

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:59.040
In a meta-analysis of algorithmic&nbsp;
approaches to predicting recidivism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:01.920
8 out of 9 of them didn’t work.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:07.440
An article in the Harvard Data Science&nbsp;
Review said we’re in “The Age of Secrecy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:15.200
and Unfairness” -- exactly the problem that&nbsp;
risk assessment tools were created to solve.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:21.440
How bad are these supposedly advanced tools? You&nbsp;
could get the same results by polling Twitter.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:26.800
Researchers from Dartmouth College showed&nbsp;
400 random volunteers on the internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:30.160
sets of 50 descriptions of defendants’ age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:35.760
sex, and prior criminal history. They predicted&nbsp;
whether each of the 50 would re-offend within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:42.320
2 years of their last crime. Their median&nbsp;
accuracy was basically the same as COMPAS.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:49.440
Reddit and Twitter are just as good as the&nbsp;
algorithm and no court would take cash money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:57.680
spongebob avatars as authoritative. Do you think&nbsp;
the Supreme Court would ignore trial by Twitter?

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:03.840
And a researcher at Duke University found that a&nbsp;
simple calculation involving a defendant’s age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:10.400
sex, and prior convictions also yielded&nbsp;
the same predictive power as COMPAS.

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:13.280
So what are we even doing?

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:19.520
Equivant has recently updated COMPAS to&nbsp;
address some of the criticism – which is good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:26.160
But what’s the real problem here? Is there a&nbsp;
limit to how we can use data as a replacement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:32.080
for personal judgment? Is human nature&nbsp;
too complex and unpredictable to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:39.200
supplanted by an equation? Are we too ignorant&nbsp;
of what leads to recidivism to build a useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:46.640
predictive model? Or are we just still&nbsp;
in the infancy of this technology, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:51.840
will AI help bridge the knowledge&nbsp;
gaps that currently ruin lives?

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:59.840
Is predicting human behavior just&nbsp;
something we can’t math our way out of?

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:05.600
And if we could… should we?

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:13.840
And as always – thanks for watching.

